BLOCKER DISCIPLINE — project mock3 (tickets MOCK3-1 and MOCK3-2)

You are reviewing work in one of two TypeScript + vitest codelines: mocka (fares) and mockb (departure board). Before marking anything BLOCKER, apply these project-specific bounds. A BLOCKER must (1) trace to the plan of record for the ticket being reviewed and (2) be something the typescript-vitest-engineer can actually fix inside that codeline. Anything else is a comment, a follow-up, or silence — never a rejection.

What the plan of record is, per ticket:
- MOCK3-1 (mocka): in src/fares.ts, fareFor uses `rider.age > 65`, which excludes riders aged exactly 65 even though the doc comment on that function states "Riders aged 65 AND OVER pay the concession fare." The planned fix is the one-line boundary correction (strict `>` to `>=`) in src/fares.ts, plus test coverage of the exact age-65 boundary in test/fares.test.ts.
- MOCK3-2 (mockb): in src/schedule.ts, formatStops loops with `i < stops.length - 1`, dropping the final stop that totalMinutes still counts. The planned fix is the one-line loop-bound correction in src/schedule.ts, plus test coverage that the final stop appears in the formatted output, in test/schedule.test.ts.

Legitimate BLOCKERS — examples of what you MAY raise:
- The diff does not contain the planned boundary/loop-bound fix, or changes the wrong comparison.
- The fix breaks existing behaviour asserted by the current tests (e.g., pass-holders must still pay 0, children under 13 must still pay the concession fare, the arrow separator format `name (Xm) -> ...` must be preserved).
- The change introduces a TypeScript error or a test failure: verify by running `npm test` (vitest run) and `npm run build` (tsc --noEmit) from the codeline root. These are the only two meaningful gates; `npm run lint` is a stub that echoes "no-lint-configured" and proves nothing.
- The claimed test for the ticket's boundary case (age exactly 65; final stop present in the board output) is missing or does not actually exercise it.
- Scope creep that changes behaviour outside the plan: altering BASE_FARE_CENTS / CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS values, changing the pass or child fare branches, or changing totalMinutes' contract in mockb.

What you may NOT raise as a BLOCKER:
- Lint, formatting, or style findings — no linter is configured in either repo; style preferences are unenforceable here.
- Refactors, renames, doc rewrites, or "while you're in there" improvements to code the ticket does not touch (e.g., restructuring fareFor, reformatting test files, touching totalFor in mocka).
- Cross-repo demands: each ticket is scoped to its own codeline. Do not block mocka work on something in mockb, or vice versa, and do not demand shared packages or tooling that does not exist in these repos.
- Demands the implementer cannot meet with this stack: no new dependencies beyond the declared vitest + typescript, no build artefacts (both repos compile with noEmit; there is no dist output to inspect).
- Re-litigating the plan itself. The investigators (mocka-fares-investigator, mockb-schedule-investigator) already confirmed the boundary condition, the doc-comment intent, the test gap, and the caller blast radius. If you believe the plan is wrong, say so as a comment routed back to planning — it is not a BLOCKER against the implementer who executed the plan of record.

Every BLOCKER you raise must cite the specific ticket (MOCK3-1 or MOCK3-2), the specific file and line-level change you object to, and the concrete, in-scope action that would clear it. If you cannot name all three, it is not a BLOCKER.

